ent plan but already completed in the registry. Since the user asked for predictions on all planned experiments from the plan, I should use the actual results from the registry rather than generate predictions for something that's already been run.\n\nNow I'm identifying all the experiments marked as planned: early-termination-simulation, compute-waste-audit, cost-effectiveness-pareto, llm-on-oh, conf1-gold-error-decomposition, cross-agent-probe-bge, cross-agent-probe-e5, 2lf-coverage-analysis, calibration-analysis, gold-power-analysis, human-ceiling-estimate, stride-sensitivity, feature-importance-figure, and trajectory-visualization. For the best tier predictions, I need to account for the constraint that with conf=1 gold data (around 282 samples), GB significantly outperforms MV on LF disagreement cases.\n\nNow I'm compiling the expected results across these experiments—LLM-on-OH should show structural methods substantially outperforming LLM approaches, the BGE and E5 probes will likely show consistent cross-agent performance below 0.65 with a large gap confirming encoder independence, the compute waste audit should demonstrate at least 40% of steps occur after stagnation with early termination saving significant compute while maintaining task success, power analysis indicates N=505 is sufficient for detecting meaningful differences, human performance ceiling around 0.88 with GB approaching that limit, and calibration metrics showing well-scaled confidence estimates. Now I'm looking at the performance metrics across different approaches and their gaps relative to human performance. I notice the calibration analysis is already complete in the registry with an ECE value of 0.0451, which falls right within my predicted range, so I can use that actual data point. Moving forward, I need to construct predictions for each planned experiment, starting with the early-termination-simulation to validate the core claims.\n\nFor the early-termination-simulation, I'm testing d