ments since some are marked as planned but might have different statuses in the registry—I'm checking through the list to see which ones are actually complete versus still pending.\n\nLooking at the data, I've got 14 planned experiments to include predictions for. For calibration-analysis, since it's already done in the registry, I'll use those actual results as the prediction instead. Now I'm pulling together the OH trajectory data to inform my predictions for compute waste and early termination—150,652 total windows, 3,409 instances, with a 21.45% stagnation rate to work with.\n\nWith OH averaging around 44 windows per trajectory and SWE at 33, and given that stagnation tends to cluster toward the end, I'm estimating that unresolved trajectories could waste 40-65% of their steps in that stagnation tail. For early termination at k=3 consecutive stagnation windows, I'd catch most genuine stagnation patterns while avoiding false positives, and the actual compute savings would hinge on when stagnation starts and how long it lingers—though task success should mostly hold since we're only affecting cases where recovery was unlikely anyway.\n\nNow I'm clarifying what \"best tier\" actually means in the evaluation framework. The definition requires that all new experiments strongly validate the anti-circularity argument with no downgraded headline claims, pushing consensus from 3/6 up to 4.5-5/6. So every experiment needs to come out favorably: the gold standard should show GB clearly outperforming MV on disagreement windows, the structural approach should dominate on OH, the BGE and E5 probes should both fail zero-shot to confirm encoder independence, and the compute waste analysis should demonstrate substantial savings.\n\nNow I'm checking the actual numbers to ensure consistency. For the conf1-gold-error-decomposition, I need to verify how many windows in the 505-item gold set B have LF-conf=1, and cross-reference this with the GB F1 scores on the full set versus the confidence-1 subset.\n\nLooking at the agreement metrics, MV's F1 on the full Set B is 0.7975, but on conf=1 windows specifically, the agreement should be lower since those are inherently less reliable. The best tier estimates suggest GB F1 around 0.75-0.82 and MV agreement around 0.70-0.75 on conf=1 gold, though I need to clarify whether \"MV agreement\" refers to raw agreement or F1 score, especially since conf=1 windows only have two LFs voting.\n\nFor the error decomposition, I'm looking at three key categories: cases where GB correctly captures signal beyond what the LFs provide, genuinely ambiguous cases where both fail, and cases where GB incorrectly overrides MV. In a well-performing tier, GB-correct-MV-wrong should substantially outweigh GB-wrong-MV-correct.\n\nNow I'm setting up the stride-sensitivity experiment with the current stride value of 1.\n\nFor the stride analysis, I'm testing stride values of 1, 3, and 5 to understand the tradeoff between data volume and sample independence. Stride 1 maximizes data (~54K windows) but creates high autocorrelation between windows, while stride 5 gives only ~11K windows but with much more independence for bootstrap confidence intervals. The key question is whether the model's conclusions hold across these different regimes despite the performance drop from reduced training data.\n\nFor the feature importance visualization, I'm planning to use SHAP or permutation importance to show which structural features like step count, unique commands, and file edit count drive the model's predictions.\n\nFor the trajectory visualization, I want to create an intuitive figure showing a timeline with LF activations overlaid and stagnation windows marked to help readers understand the patterns.\n\nI'm realizing the calibration analysis is already complete with actual results showing ECE values well within the target range, so I can present those directly rather than planning future work.\n\nNow I need to finalize the YAML structure